        .prose
          Fragment:md
            ## Installation

            NMBL integrates with Svelte through a preprocessor. Install it as a dev dependency:

            ```bash
            # Using npm
            npm install --save-dev @nmbl-lang/svelte

            # Using bun
            bun add -d @nmbl-lang/svelte

            # Using pnpm
            pnpm add -D @nmbl-lang/svelte
            ```

            ## Configuration

            Add the NMBL preprocessor to your `svelte.config.js`:

            ```js title="svelte.config.js"
            import { nmblPreprocess } from '@nmbl-lang/svelte';

            export default {
              preprocess: [
                nmblPreprocess(),
              ],
            };
            ```

            Your `vite.config.ts` needs no NMBL-specific changes — the standard Svelte plugin is all that's required; the preprocessor handles NMBL:

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
            import { svelte } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';

            export default defineConfig({
              plugins: [svelte()],
            });
            ```

            ## Basic Usage

            Add `lang="nmbl"` to a `<template>` block in your `.svelte` component, then write the markup as NMBL. Everything else (script with Svelte 5 runes, styles) stays the same:

            ```svelte title="App.svelte"
            <script lang="ts">
              let count = $state(0);
              let items = $state(['Apple', 'Banana', 'Cherry']);
            </script>

            <template lang="nmbl">
            div#app
              h1 NMBL + Svelte
              button(onclick={() => count++}) Clicked {count} times
              ul
                @each(item, i of items :key="item")
                  li {i + 1}. {item}
            </template>

            <style>
              #app { font-family: sans-serif; }
            </style>
            ```

            Interpolation uses Svelte's single-brace `{expr}` syntax. Svelte directives like `bind:value`, `onclick`, and `class:active` all pass straight through.

            ## Control Flow

            NMBL's `@if` / `@elseif` / `@else` and `@each` blocks compile to Svelte's native `{#if}` and `{#each}` blocks:

            ```nmbl
            @if(loggedIn)
              p Welcome back, {user.name}
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(item, i of items :key="item")
                li {i + 1}. {item}
            ```

            `@if` becomes `{#if …}…{/if}`, and `@each(item, i of items :key="item")` becomes `{#each items as item, i (item)}`. Conditions and loop expressions pass straight through, so you write them exactly as in Svelte. Svelte also gets a few extras that map to its block syntax: `@await`, `@key`, and `@snippet`, plus `{@html …}` for raw HTML. Plain Svelte blocks still work too if you prefer them.

            ## Comments

            `//` comments are stripped from the output (dev notes); `//!` comments are rendered as real HTML comments:

            ```nmbl
            // dev note — never shipped
            //! becomes <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            You can also comment out individual props inside a tag's parens — handy while debugging:

            ```nmbl
            button(
              type="submit"
              // disabled
              /* aria-label="Save" */
            ) Save
            ```

            ## Editor Support

            Svelte editor support comes from `@nmbl-lang/language-server` (a Volar 2.4 server) wired through the NMBL VS Code extension. It activates only on `.svelte` files that contain a `<template lang="nmbl">` block — other Svelte files are left untouched.

          .features-grid
            .feature
              h4 🎨 Syntax Highlighting
              p NMBL syntax is highlighted inside template lang="nmbl" blocks
            .feature
              h4 💡 HTML Completions
              p Tag names, attribute names, and values are suggested via the embedded HTML language service
            .feature
              h4 🔗 Go to Definition
              p Tokens map back to your NMBL source, so navigation works on the original file
            .feature
              h4 ❗ Syntax Diagnostics
              p NMBL syntax errors and unsupported control-flow blocks are surfaced inline

          Fragment:md
            > **Note:** Deep, framework-type-aware checking of expressions inside `@if` / `@each` is not yet available for Svelte — the expressions compile to Svelte syntax but aren't run through a TypeScript type-checker. For the fullest type intellisense today, Vue is the most complete via its dedicated Volar plugin.

            ## Troubleshooting

          .troubleshooting:md
            ### Template not transforming

            Make sure `nmblPreprocess()` is listed in your `svelte.config.js` `preprocess` array, and restart the dev server after editing the config.

            ### Syntax errors with indentation

            NMBL uses indentation for structure. Your root element must start at column 0 with no leading whitespace, and use consistent 2-space indentation (no tabs).

            ### Editor shows no highlighting

            Install the NMBL VS Code extension (syntax highlighting + language server). It activates only on `.svelte` files that contain a `<template lang="nmbl">` block.

